+/**
+ * HexaTech Time Planner sold on its own like HexaTech CRM (owner, 2026-11-09: "make it a module sold separately like CRM
+ * and also included in the enterprise plan"): the staff planner with its AI — day and week plans by person, the backlog
+ * and the open pool, AI plans, reviews, capture and voice. Lite, Standard and Pro at CRM's prices, 15 % less a month
+ * paid yearly, the 7-day trial. The loyalty application (App\Support\PlanModules) names the plans:
+ *
+ *   time_lite 4 900, time 8 500, time_pro 14 900 (cents a month), and the Time Planner with Appointments, Loyalty,
+ *   Content or CRM in every pair of packages (`appointments-time`, `crm_pro-time_lite`, `content-time_pro`…) at the sum
+ *   of the two. The Time Planner comes last in a slug (the loyalty application's module order). No plan of three
+ *   products: that is the full suite.
+ *
+ * The plans hold the new product `time_planner` (a module product: the loyalty application reads a plan whose products
+ * are all module products as an organisation on modules). Every package carries the `time_management` switch — the
+ * Enterprise plan's, which the loyalty application's planner endpoints are behind; Enterprise keeps it as before — and
+ * they differ by team (`max_team_members`: 3, 10, 25) and the monthly AI allowance its plans, reviews and captures spend
+ * (`ai_monthly_cost_cents`: 5, 15, 40 USD). Two products that spend AI add their allowances (one budget).
+ *
+ * Like the module plans before them they are HIDDEN and unpriced. Sell them, once, in this application's environment
+ * (it prices every plan whose metadata names modules, and reuses the prices it already made):
+ *
+ *   php artisan stripe:sync --module-plans --publish
+ *
+ * A plan that is already sold (public or with a Stripe price) is left as its owner set it, and logged; a hidden, unpriced
+ * one under the same slug takes these terms and keeps only these products. Idempotent.
+ */
